I cannot reproduce a one-way connectivity problem. End-to-end IP connectivity is healthy in both directions. From the hosts, bidirectional pings succeed with 0% loss and ~1.2–1.4 ms RTTs (pc1↔pc2 reachability check). Throughput is symmetric and stable: iperf from pc1→pc2 and pc2→pc1 both sustain ~120–130 Mbit/s with minimal retransmits. Routing is correct on both sides: router1 has a BGP-learned route to 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2, and router2 has a BGP-learned route to 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1. FRR BGP sessions are established on both routers, each receiving the opposite LAN prefix (show ip bgp summary shows 1 prefix received per peer). Host defaults are set to their local routers (pc1 via 195.11.14.1, pc2 via 200.1.1.1), and all L2 interfaces are up.

Affected component: none observed; L3 routing and L2 links are functioning.
Mechanism: Since both prefixes are exchanged via BGP and installed, return traffic follows the expected reverse path without loss.
Localization: Verified on router1 eth0/eth1 and router2 eth0/eth1, plus pc1/pc2 IP stacks.
Root cause: No fault present in this lab at the time of testing; the reported one-way issue is not reproducible.
Corrective fix: No network changes required. If the issue recurs, capture packets simultaneously on pc1 and router1 eth0 to confirm replies returning, and check for any transient upstream filtering or application-layer timeouts on the partner side. 
